WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.020
Least privilege in Bash is a repeatable engineering habit.

2
00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:07.640
Start by defining the job before granting access.

3
00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:17.900
Know what the script should do, what systems it touches, which files it reads and writes, which commands it calls, what data it handles, and which actions are explicitly out of scope.

4
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:20.480
Clear purpose makes access review possible.

5
00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:24.420
Without clear purpose, broad permission begins to look normal.

6
00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:27.100
Use dedicated identities where practical.

7
00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:35.040
Grant only the files, commands, services, networks, and credentials required for the task.

8
00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:43.620
Separate read, write, execute, delete, and administer permissions instead of granting them as one broad package.

9
00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:53.080
Use groups carefully, review created-file permissions, and avoid relying on root or broad administrator identities as the default way to make scripts work.

10
00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:55.280
Keep privileged actions narrow.

11
00:00:55.380 --> 00:01:06.340
Separate ordinary work from elevated work, scope sudo rules carefully, protect secrets, and use approved mechanisms for tokens, keys, and automation credentials.

12
00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:15.320
Control runtime context by reviewing PATH, environment variables, working directory assumptions, external tools, and helper scripts.

13
00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:28.300
In automated environments, review CI/CD jobs, cron entries, systemd services, containers, and remote execution identities individually rather than using one powerful identity everywhere.

14
00:01:28.580 --> 00:01:32.020
Finally, keep least privilege alive over time.

15
00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:38.040
Log identity, target, action, time, and result without exposing secrets.

16
00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:41.580
Monitor permission failures and unusual access patterns.

17
00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:49.360
Review accounts, groups, sudo paths, keys, tokens, CI/CD variables, and remote access.

18
00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:55.300
Rotate credentials, revoke retired access, and improve legacy scripts incrementally.

19
00:01:55.400 --> 00:02:01.320
Least privilege makes Bash automation safer, more explainable, and easier to trust when the work matters.
